= Solution
{parent=3}

A singular two-dimensional <affine toric variety> in the normal fan construction must come from a full-dimensional pointed cone; cones of smaller dimension give smooth charts with a torus factor. Let its primitive boundary generators be $v_0,v_{t+1}$. Choose an oriented <lattice basis> with $v_0=(1,0)$, and write the other generator as $(a,r)$, $r>0$. Primitivity gives $\gcd(a,r)=1$. Changing the second basis vector by a multiple of the first lets us write the cone as
$$
\sigma=\mathbb R_{\geq0}(1,0)+\mathbb R_{\geq0}(-q,r),\qquad 0<q<r,\quad\gcd(q,r)=1.
$$
Here $r>1$ because determinant one would give a smooth chart. The sublattice $N'=\mathbb Z(1,0)+\mathbb Z(-q,r)$ has index $r$, and its affine chart is $\mathbb C^2$. The quotient $N/N'$ is cyclic, generated by $(0,1)$, whose coordinates in the two boundary generators are $(q/r,1/r)$. Thus the kernel of the corresponding finite map of tori acts on the two affine coordinates as $(x,y)\mapsto(\zeta^qx,\zeta y)$. More explicitly, invariant monomials are exactly those $x^iy^j$ with $qi+j\equiv0\pmod r$, which are the elements of the original dual lattice in the first quadrant. Their ring is $\mathbb C[\sigma^\vee\cap M]$. Hence **the singularity is the cyclic quotient**
$$
\boxed{U_\sigma\cong\mathbb C^2/\mu_r= X_{r,q}.}
$$
The choice of the two boundary axes is unordered; exchanging them replaces $q$ by its inverse modulo $r$.

The <Hirzebruch–Jung resolution> is computed by the negative Euclidean algorithm. Put $r_0=r$, $r_1=q$ and recursively set
$$
b_i=\left\lceil\frac{r_{i-1}}{r_i}\right\rceil,\qquad r_{i+1}=b_ir_i-r_{i-1}.
$$
The nonzero remainders strictly decrease, $0\le r_{i+1}<r_i$, and their greatest common divisor stays one; the process terminates with $r_t=1$, $r_{t+1}=0$. All $b_i\ge2$, and unwinding the recursion gives the <negative continued fraction>
$$
\frac rq=[b_1,\ldots,b_t]^-:=b_1-\frac1{b_2-\displaystyle\frac1{\cdots-1/b_t}}.
$$
Start with $v_0=(1,0)$ and $v_1=(0,1)$ and define $v_{i+1}=b_iv_i-v_{i-1}$. The determinant of every consecutive pair is one, by induction. Moreover
$$
\det(v_i,(-q,r))=r_i,
$$
again by the same recursion and the initial values $r,q$. Each intermediate ray is therefore inside $\sigma$. To check the ordering explicitly, the first coordinates after $v_1$ are negative, the second coordinates positive, and successive determinants are positive. At the last step the determinant with the endpoint is zero. The vector $v_{t+1}$ is primitive, points in the same direction as $(-q,r)$, and hence equals it. Subdivide by all the intermediate rays $v_1,\ldots,v_t$. Every new cone is regular, and the subdivision has the same support, so it gives a proper smooth resolution.

Each internal ray has a compact invariant curve $E_i\cong\mathbb P^1$, because the quotient fan at it has the two opposite primitive rays. The relation $v_{i-1}+v_{i+1}=b_iv_i$ gives its self-intersection $E_i^2=-b_i$. This can also be checked directly: on the chart for $(v_{i-1},v_i)$ use coordinates $(x,y)$ with $E_i=\{y=0\}$; on the next chart the coordinates are $(u,v)=(x^{b_i}y,x^{-1})$, with $E_i=\{u=0\}$. The normal-coordinate transition is that of $\mathcal O_{\mathbb P^1}(-b_i)$, proving the self-intersection. Neighboring exceptional curves meet transversely once and nonneighbors do not meet.

Since $b_i\ge2$, there is no exceptional minus-one curve. Combinatorially, deleting $v_i$ gives a cone of determinant $b_i>1$. Deleting a longer consecutive block cannot give a regular cone either: determinants of successively more distant rays satisfy the same recursion with coefficients at least two and grow from $0,1$ to integers at least two. Thus no proper smooth coarsening of this subdivision exists. This proves the minimality of the <minimal resolution of a cyclic quotient surface singularity>; equivalently its exceptional chain has no curve that can be blown down to a smooth point. **The number of exceptional curves is the length $t$ of the negative continued fraction.**

If $t=1$, then $r/q=b_1$ is an integer, so coprimality gives $q=1$. Conversely $q=1$ gives the single coefficient $[r]^-$ and one curve. If $t=2$, then
$$
\frac rq=b_1-\frac1{b_2}=\frac{b_1b_2-1}{b_2}.
$$
The numerator and denominator on the right are coprime, so $r=b_1b_2-1$ and $q=b_2\ge2$. Therefore $q>1$ and $q\mid r+1$. Conversely, if these conditions hold, then $b_1=(r+1)/q\ge2$, $b_2=q\ge2$ give exactly this two-term expansion. Hence **the requested equivalences are**
$$
\boxed{t=1\iff q=1,\qquad t=2\iff q>1\text{ and }q\mid r+1.}
$$

Now consider a <complete fan> in dimension two with three rays and at most one singularity. Its three two-dimensional cones are the consecutive ray pairs. At least two cones are regular, and they share a ray. After an oriented lattice change, take their consecutive generators to be $v_1=(1,0)$, $v_2=(0,1)$ and $v_3=(-1,b)$: the first cone is a basis, and $\det(v_2,v_3)=1$ forces the first coordinate $-1$. Completeness forces $b<0$, because the third pair must span the missing sector with positive determinant $\det(v_3,v_1)=-b$. Write $b=-r$, $r\ge1$. The unique positive primitive relation is $v_1+rv_2+v_3=0$, so the fan is that of the <weighted projective plane> $\mathbb P(1,r,1)$, or $\mathbb P(1,1,r)$ after permuting weights. For $r=1$ this is $\mathbb P^2$; for $r>1$ its remaining cone has type $X_{r,1}$. This proves **the full three-ray classification**
$$
\boxed{X_\Sigma\cong\mathbb P(1,1,r),\quad r\ge1.}
$$
These are the <complete three-ray toric surfaces with at most one singular point>, including the smooth case.

For four rays and exactly the specified singularity, place the three regular cones consecutively. Normalize the middle one to have generators $(1,0),(0,1)$. Its neighboring regular cones then force the cyclic ray list
$$
v_1=(a,-1),\quad v_2=(1,0),\quad v_3=(0,1),\quad v_4=(-1,b).
$$
The remaining cone has determinant $\det(v_4,v_1)=1-ab=r$, so $ab=1-r$. Thus $a,b$ have opposite signs, and $s=|a|$ is a positive divisor of $r-1$. In the oriented lattice basis $v_4,(0,-1)$,
$$
v_1=-a\,v_4+r(0,-1),
$$
so the singular cone has quotient parameter $a\bmod r$. It follows that its parameter is congruent to $\pm s$. If identification with the specified singularity exchanges the two boundary rays, the parameter is inverted; this does not change the criterion, since for $st=r-1$ we have $s^{-1}\equiv-t\pmod r$, and $t$ is the complementary positive factor. The same identity with a minus sign handles $(-s)^{-1}$.

Conversely suppose $s\mid r-1$ and $s\equiv\varepsilon q\pmod r$, $\varepsilon\in\{1,-1\}$. Choose $a=\varepsilon s$ and $b=-\varepsilon(r-1)/s$ in the displayed four rays. They are primitive and cyclically ordered, with all four gaps less than a half-plane; the opposite signs of $a,b$ ensure completeness. Three consecutive determinants are one, and the fourth is $r$. Its quotient parameter is $a\equiv q\pmod r$, so the only singular point has the required type. We have proved the <four-ray completion criterion for a cyclic quotient surface singularity>:
$$
\boxed{\exists\text{ such complete four-ray surface}\iff\exists s>0:\ s\mid r-1,\ s\equiv\pm q\pmod r.}
$$

= 4
{parent=Paper 12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

Let $v_i$ be the primitive lattice generator of the ray $\tau_i$. In the <toric divisor class sequence>, the first map sends a <torus character> exponent $m\in M$ to its <principal divisor on a toric variety>:
$$
m\longmapsto\operatorname{div}(\chi^m)=\sum_i\langle m,v_i\rangle F_i.
$$
The second map sends an invariant <Weil divisor> to its class modulo principal divisors. Completeness makes the rays span $N_{\mathbb R}$, so the first map is injective. These are the maps in the given sequence; its exactness need not be reproved.

For $D=\sum_i r_iF_i$, let
$$
P_D=\{m\in M_{\mathbb R}:\langle m,v_i\rangle\ge-r_i\text{ for all }i\}.
$$
The <torus characters> $\chi^m$ with $m\in P_D\cap M$ form a basis of $\mathcal L(D)=\{f\in\mathbb C(X):\operatorname{div}(f)+D\ge0\}\cup\{0\}$. This works for Weil divisors, not only for Cartier divisors. It is the character basis associated to the <lattice polytope of a toric divisor>.

For a Cartier divisor, the <toric ampleness criterion> is as follows. Each maximal cone $\sigma$ has local Cartier data $m_\sigma\in M$ satisfying $\langle m_\sigma,v_i\rangle=-r_i$ on its rays. The divisor is ample exactly when
$$
\langle m_\sigma,v_j\rangle>-r_j\qquad\text{for every ray }\tau_j\not\subseteq\sigma.
$$
Equivalently the support function is strictly concave, with these linear pieces. For a Q-Cartier Weil divisor use rational $m_\sigma$ and clear their denominators; the criterion means that a positive multiple is ample Cartier. An arbitrary Weil divisor need not be Q-Cartier, so this qualification cannot be omitted.

Take positive weights $q=(q_0,\ldots,q_n)$, reduced so their common greatest common divisor is one. A toric definition of <weighted projective space> uses
$$
\boxed{N=\mathbb Z^{n+1}/\mathbb Zq,\qquad v_i=\overline e_i,\qquad\Sigma=\{\operatorname{cone}(v_i:i\in I):I\subsetneq\{0,\ldots,n\}\}.}
$$
If the weights are well-formed, meaning $\gcd(q_0,\ldots,\widehat{q_i},\ldots,q_n)=1$ for every $i$, the $v_i$ are primitive: the quotient $N/\mathbb Zv_i$ has torsion of order that omitted-weight greatest common divisor. The lattice $N$ itself is free because $q$ is primitive. The positive relation $\sum_iq_iv_i=0$ implies that proper subsets generate pointed cones and that they meet along common faces. Completeness can be seen directly: represent a vector by real coordinates $a_i$ and subtract $\min_i(a_i/q_i)$ times $q$. The resulting coordinates are nonnegative and at least one is zero, placing the vector in one of the displayed cones. Their common-face property follows from this unique normalization of each class modulo $\mathbb Rq$. This gives the fan of the <well-formed weighted projective space>.

The dual lattice is
$$
M=\{m\in\mathbb Z^{n+1}:q\mathbin{\cdot}m=0\}.
$$
Its map in the divisor sequence is simply inclusion in $\mathbb Z^{n+1}$, so the homomorphism $a\mapsto q\mathbin{\cdot}a$ identifies the cokernel with $\mathbb Z$. It is onto by coprimality of all the weights. Thus
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Cl}(X)\cong\mathbb Z,\qquad [F_i]=q_iH.}
$$
Choose integers $a_i$ by the Bezout identity $\sum_iq_ia_i=1$. Then $D=\sum_i a_iF_i$ explicitly represents the positive generator $H$.

To verify ampleness in the Weil/Q-Cartier sense, put $\ell=\operatorname{lcm}(q_0,\ldots,q_n)$. On the maximal cone omitting ray $j$, define
$$
m_j=\frac{\ell}{q_j}e_j-\ell a\in M.
$$
This is integral and has $q\mathbin{\cdot}m_j=\ell-\ell=0$. Its pairings with the included rays are $-\ell a_i$, while on the omitted ray they equal $-\ell a_j+\ell/q_j>-\ell a_j$. Consequently $\ell D$ is Cartier and ample by the numerical criterion. This is an explicit <class-group generator of a well-formed weighted projective space>.

**The generator is generally ample Weil, not Cartier.** If the source's word “ample” is interpreted as requiring a Cartier divisor, its generator request is false in general: on $\mathbb P(1,1,2)$, take $D=F_0$. On the chart omitting the weight-two ray, the Cartier equations force $m=(-1,0,1/2)$, which is not in the integral dual lattice. Twice this datum is integral, so the class-one divisor has Cartier index two and no Cartier divisor generates its class group. With the usual ample-Q-Cartier-Weil interpretation, the construction above proves exactly the requested result. This distinction also explains the use of the divisorial spaces $\mathcal L(rD)$ rather than assuming an invertible sheaf $\mathcal O(D)$.

Finally let an ample divisor represent the class-group generator. Its class is the positive generator; the negative generator cannot be ample because a positive Cartier multiple has negative degree on the curves where the positive generator has positive degree. After replacing by a linearly equivalent invariant divisor, write it as $D=\sum a_iF_i$ with $\sum q_ia_i=1$. A character basis element of $\mathcal L(rD)$ has exponent $m\in M$ with $m_i+ra_i\ge0$. Set $b_i=m_i+ra_i$. Then $b\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}^{n+1}$ and $\sum q_ib_i=r$. Conversely every nonnegative exponent vector of weighted degree $r$ gives the unique $m=b-ra\in M$. Therefore
$$
\chi^m\in\mathcal L(rD)\longmapsto X_0^{m_0+ra_0}\cdots X_n^{m_n+ra_n}
$$
is a bijection of bases in each degree. Adding $m$ and $r$ under multiplication adds these nonnegative exponent vectors, so it preserves products and the unit. A different linearly equivalent representative changes the basis by the corresponding rational-function power and gives the same graded-ring isomorphism. Hence **the divisorial section ring is the weighted polynomial ring**:
$$
\boxed{\bigoplus_{r\ge0}\mathcal L(rD)\cong k[X_0,\ldots,X_n],\qquad\deg X_i=q_i.}
$$
The character construction is defined over the ground field $k$, so this is an isomorphism of graded $k$-algebras. It proves the <divisorial section ring of a well-formed weighted projective space> assertion even when its ample generator is not Cartier.
